## Scope and source of truth

This review uses the exported report records, tenant directory, desk notes, and the functions
that build the desk's tables. It is an export snapshot, not a claim about the current queue.
The build date is not a later data-refresh date. Rebuild with a fresh export before using
these counts as current.

## Engineering update — 5 October 2026

The [Challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now reports
“#4471 · closed 05 oct”:

- **Parser: fixed.** Engineering reports `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **Hint: won't fix.** The booking-v3 form was retired; the desk does not report a hint fix.

These are different resolutions. A retired form is not evidence that its hint was repaired.
The outcomes are reported by the desk, not independently verified by our local rendering
tests. The export counts below remain historical; this update adds no new report records.

## Separate tickets

At export time, the parser read numeric dates month first. For day-first tenants, an intended day/month
value can become a month/day value. This affects app and email reports, and booking-v2
form reports. Engineering now reports a locale-aware parser fix, not just a different label.

The other path was booking-v3's form: its hint said MM/DD/YYYY while the form interpreted
input in the tenant's day-first order. A repair would need the hint and interpretation to
agree. Engineering instead retired the form. The parser fix did not repair that hint.

The table uses tenant locale and booking-form version, not browser locale or intake tags.
Locale order is obtained through Intl.DateTimeFormat, not a hard-coded country list.
Duplicate export rows count once by report ID; different IDs remain distinct reports,
including corrections or follow-ups. Counts describe reports, not affected people.

<IncidentReview />

## Why the merge hid this

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) accepted subject-only deduplication.
The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) still tells the desk to merge matching subjects and to
merge when uncertain. Its interactive example already compares tenant/form causes instead.
The words and executable demonstration disagree.

Subject similarity can help locate candidates, but it cannot establish a shared cause.
Read the body, resolve the tenant's locale and form, and compare the actual failure path.
Matching subjects can conceal different failures; different subjects can describe the same
failure. Missing tenant information or conflicting symptoms need investigation, not a
forced merge into the largest ticket.

Do not rewrite accepted decision 0001. A replacement decision should record why subject-only
merging failed, the evidence needed before merging, and how unresolved cases stay visible.

## Received is not merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart separately counts when reports arrived and when the desk merged them.
A merge backlog is not a sudden increase in new reports. The export records reports from
April onward, including later reports after the initial merge. Known is not fixed:
customers still describe missed visits, manual rescheduling, and workarounds.

## Desk handoff

Keep the parser and booking-v3 hint tickets separate. Keep the evidence exceptions visible
inside the parser investigation; their current routing is not proof of their mechanism.
Ask for the original input, intended date, saved value, rendered invite, tenant configuration,
timezone, and a reproducible sequence before declaring another root cause or a completed fix.

The proposed triage change and this review do not alter the live ticket system.
The desk reports #4471 closed, with a parser fix and a separate won't-fix outcome for the
retired form. Closing this review does not establish the mechanism of every unexplained
report. Recheck the current desk before quoting these historical numbers as current.

